#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 660.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125630} :activity/id #uuid "304b28d7-9caa-5437-822d-7b03dc2aef7f" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049676 :topic/id #uuid "0a177390-6525-5d0c-8721-f7f75db39408" :topic/math-academy-id 1945} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125485 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125816 :tutorial/content "So, given a stationary critical point $\\mathbf{a} \\in \\mathbb{R}^n$ of a function $f : \\mathbb{R}^n \\to \\mathbb{R},$ how do we classify it?\n\nOne way is to consider the Taylor expansion of $f$ about $\\mathbf{a}{:}$\n\n$$\nf(\\mathbf{x}) = f(\\mathbf{a}) + \\boldsymbol{f}'(\\mathbf{a})(\\mathbf{x} - \\mathbf{a}) + \\dfrac12(\\mathbf{x}-\\mathbf{a})^T \\boldsymbol{f}''(\\mathbf{a})(\\mathbf{x} - \\mathbf{a}) + \\cdots\n$$\n\nSince $\\mathbf{a}$ is a stationary point, we have $\\boldsymbol{f}'(\\mathbf{a}) = \\mathbf 0^T.$ This gives\n\n$$\nf(\\mathbf{x}) = f(\\mathbf{a})  + \\dfrac12(\\mathbf{x}-\\mathbf{a})^T \\boldsymbol{f}''(\\mathbf{a})(\\mathbf{x} - \\mathbf{a}) + \\cdots.\n$$\n\nLet's assume that $\\mathbf x$ is close to $\\mathbf a,$ $\\det\\left(\\boldsymbol f''(\\mathbf a)\\right) \\neq 0,$ and that the terms of cubic order and higher are sufficiently small. Then, we have\n\n$$\nf(\\mathbf{x}) \\approx f(\\mathbf{a})  + \\dfrac12(\\mathbf{x}-\\mathbf{a})^T \\boldsymbol{f}''(\\mathbf{a})(\\mathbf{x} - \\mathbf{a})\n$$\n\nwhich we can rewrite as\n\n$$\nf(\\mathbf{x}) - f(\\mathbf{a}) \\approx \\dfrac12(\\mathbf{x}-\\mathbf{a})^T \\boldsymbol{f}''(\\mathbf{a})(\\mathbf{x} - \\mathbf{a}).\n$$\n\nRecall that $\\boldsymbol{f}''(\\mathbf{a})$ is an $n \\times n$ *symmetric* matrix. Thus, the term\n\n$$\n(\\mathbf{x}-\\mathbf{a})^T \\boldsymbol{f}''(\\mathbf{a})(\\mathbf{x} - \\mathbf{a})\n$$\n\nis a *quadratic form*.\n\nTherefore, we note the following:\n\n- If $\\boldsymbol{f}''(\\mathbf{a})$ is positive definite, this quadratic form is always positive, and\n\n$$\nf(\\mathbf{x}) - f(\\mathbf{a}) \\approx \\dfrac12(\\mathbf{x} - \\mathbf{a})^T \\boldsymbol{f}''(\\mathbf{a})(\\mathbf{x} - \\mathbf{a}) > 0\n\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\nf(\\mathbf{x}) > f(\\mathbf{a})\n$$\n\nfor all $\\mathbf{x}$ sufficiently close to $\\mathbf{a}.$ Hence, $\\mathbf{a}$ is a local *minimum*.\n\n- If $\\boldsymbol{f}''(\\mathbf{a})$ is negative definite, this quadratic form is always negative, and\n\n$$\nf(\\mathbf{x}) - f(\\mathbf{a}) \\approx \\dfrac12(\\mathbf{x} - \\mathbf{a})^T \\boldsymbol{f}''(\\mathbf{a})(\\mathbf{x} - \\mathbf{a}) < 0\n\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\nf(\\mathbf{x}) < f(\\mathbf{a})\n$$\n\nfor all $\\mathbf{x}$ sufficiently close to $\\mathbf{a}.$ Hence, $\\mathbf{a}$ is a local *maximum.*\n\n- If this quadratic form is neither positive-definite nor negative-definite, then $\\mathbf{a}$ is a saddle point.\n\nThe assumption $\\det\\left(\\boldsymbol f''(\\mathbf a)\\right) \\neq 0$ is important. If $\\det\\left(\\boldsymbol f''(\\mathbf a)\\right) =0,$ then the quadratic form is zero along some line through $\\mathbf a$ or zero *everywhere* in a neighborhood of $\\mathbf a.$ In such cases, we cannot ignore the higher-order terms and must use another method to classify the critical point.\n\nIn the work that follows, we will only consider cases where $\\det\\left(\\boldsymbol f''(\\mathbf a)\\right) \\neq 0.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "3cedcd0c-77c7-5789-b76d-7ae3307c237b" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4263 :tutorial/title "Classifying Local Extrema Using Taylor Polynomials"} :step/id #uuid "f3f90cb9-acd8-5630-859d-3bd37e502189" :step/math-academy-id 25915 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125645}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125486 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125817 :tutorial/content "Recall that the *derivative* of a multivariable function $f:\\mathbb R^n\\to\\mathbb R$ at $\\mathbf x = \\mathbf a$ is given by the matrix\n\n![](images/b5/b5dfe5fbad89ef3c6231dafe40d719be1faf007f782342abd6475756eda97b1e.svg)\n\nAnalogously with functions of one variable, we have the following definitions:\n\n- A point $\\mathbf x = \\mathbf a$ is a **critical point** of $f$ if $\\boldsymbol f'(\\mathbf a) = \\mathbf 0^T$ or does not exist.\n\n- Critical points where $\\boldsymbol f'(\\mathbf a) = \\mathbf 0^T$ are known as **stationary points.**\n\nThe local extrema of a function $f$ occur at the critical points. So, we can find the local extrema of $f$ by determining the stationary points *and* the points where $\\boldsymbol f'(\\mathbf x)$ does not exist and classify them accordingly (we'll discuss how to do this shortly). \n\nNot all of the solutions of $\\boldsymbol f'(\\mathbf a) = \\mathbf 0^T$ give rise to local extrema! Solutions to $\\boldsymbol f'(\\mathbf a) = \\mathbf 0^T$ that *do not* give rise to local extreme values are called **saddle points**.\n\nFor example, consider the hyperbolic paraboloid \n\n$$\nz = f(x,y) = y^2-x^2\n$$\n\nwhose graph is shown below.\n\n![](images/ce/cea092da684db6b055c78369fcb987c3577a027ef0d8f03d598e115e85775ff5.png)\n\nIt's easy to check that $\\boldsymbol f'(0,0) = \\mathbf 0^T,$ and so $(0,0)$ is a stationary point of $f.$ This point is a saddle point. Note that the surface curves up in one direction (the red curve) and down in a different direction (the green curve), resembling a horse riding saddle.\n\nIn any neighborhood of a saddle point $\\mathbf a,$ there are points $\\mathbf{x}$ and $\\mathbf{x}'$ such that $\\mathbf{x}-\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{x}'-\\mathbf{a}$ are orthogonal and they satisfy\n\n$$\nf(\\mathbf{x}) - f(\\mathbf a) >0 \\quad \\textrm{and} \\quad f(\\mathbf{x}') - f(\\mathbf a) <0.\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "60d15795-6c7a-5ec9-806d-625f5d8c5c85" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4264 :tutorial/title "Saddle Points"} :step/id #uuid "42eecd67-0de9-5101-84f7-5ef560683d60" :step/math-academy-id 25917 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125485}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125487 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124570 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124379 :question/id #uuid "c6938151-ab77-5288-ba08-156dc942ddec" :question/math-academy-id "e-7030"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "40d58ee6-1121-5ddc-a96f-1a7fa7a193dd" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124821} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124822} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284418} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284419} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284423} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284424} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186367881} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186367888} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186434754} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186434755} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186434759} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186434764}] :knowledge-point/title "Classifying Local Extrema Using Sylvester's Criterion"} :step/id #uuid "98e2abbe-ffee-53fc-af20-297624f9d77a" :step/math-academy-id 25918} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125625 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124646 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124209 :question/id #uuid "137818ca-6c48-5ae1-8788-2015f7890ad5" :question/math-academy-id "e-12460"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3ee9cbe8-ad40-5155-b42d-1802f36adf27" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048164}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124811} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124812} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284436} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284450} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186398583} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186401426} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186433377} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186433378} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186433379} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186433380} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186434758} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186434762}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Global Extrema of a Multivariable Function"} :step/id #uuid "1bf38d19-ac54-50d2-9456-e7e1e26fc0a6" :step/math-academy-id 44845 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125628}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125626 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124647 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124210 :question/id #uuid "976e4ec4-93f9-57a4-9f2b-2116e09fed22" :question/math-academy-id "e-12461"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f0fb118f-c1cc-51fd-a8cc-c29cf705acfe" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050033}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124813} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124814} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235544} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284447} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284448} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284449} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186401439} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186434756} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186434757} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186434761} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186434763} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186434765} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186445198}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Local Extrema of a Multivariable Function"} :step/id #uuid "4b1acac2-a584-5026-9cb7-d5e4ae42f3ee" :step/math-academy-id 44846 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125486}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125628 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125873 :tutorial/content "Now, recall the definition of local extrema for single-variable functions:\n\n- A function $f(x)$ has a local minimum at $x=a$ if $f(x) \\geq f(a)$ for all $x$-values in a neighborhood of $a.$\n\n- A function $f(x)$ has a local maximum at $x=a$ if $f(x) \\leq f(a)$ for all $x$-values in a neighborhood of $a.$\n\nOn the function's graph, a local minimum is the lowest point of a valley, and a local maximum is the highest point of a peak.\n\n![](images/ba/ba5be082363bd963514baa6b1a777f02098d53a3b0a80ddc2fad231e4669ebd7.png)\n\nWe can generalize the concept of local extrema to multivariable functions $f:\\mathbb R^n\\to\\mathbb R$ as follows:\n\n- A multivariable function $f(\\mathbf{x})$ with domain $D \\subseteq \\mathbb{R}^n$ has a local minimum at the point $\\mathbf{a} \\in D$ if $f(\\mathbf{x}) \\geq f(\\mathbf{a})$ for all points $\\mathbf{x}$ in a neighborhood of $\\mathbf{a}.$\n\n- A multivariable function $f(\\mathbf{x})$ with domain $D \\subseteq \\mathbb{R}^n$ has a local maximum at the point $\\mathbf{a} \\in D$ if $f(\\mathbf{x}) \\leq f(\\mathbf{a})$ for all points $\\mathbf{x}$ in a neighborhood of $\\mathbf{a}.$\n\nIn particular, for a function $f: \\mathbb{R}^2 \\to \\mathbb{R},$ local minima and maxima are the lowest points in valleys and highest points in peaks, respectively, in the graph $z=f(x,y)$ of the function.\n\n![](images/74/74d04165fd3bd5cd24ae82351bbf840e7d549b272c3deea16671dbfcd40bc75a.png)\n\nFor the points $A$ and $B,$ we can take neighborhoods of the $(x,y)$-values at these points such that $A$ and $B$ are the lowest points on the graph in these neighborhoods. Hence, they are local minima.\n\nSimilarly, for the points $C$ and $D,$ we can take neighborhoods of the $(x,y)$-values at these points such that $C$ and $D$ are the highest points on the graph in these neighborhoods. Hence, they are local maxima.\n\n**Note**: For the point $E,$ there are points $(x,y)$ in the surrounding regions such that $f(x_E,y_E) = f(x,y).$ However, since the definition of local extrema is non-strict, $f(\\mathbf{x}) \\geq f(\\mathbf{a})$ or $f(\\mathbf{x}) \\leq f(\\mathbf{a})$, the point $E$ satisfies the conditions for a local minimum *and* a local maximum.\n\nFinally, the points $F$ and $G$ are neither local minima nor local maxima. Notice that any neighborhood of $F$ will contain both points that lie higher than $F$ and lower than $F$ on the graph (similarly, for the point $G$)." :tutorial/id #uuid "fc6789c5-925e-54f4-9a5a-bfb179e77937" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7752 :tutorial/title "Local Extrema of a Multivariable Function"} :step/id #uuid "086acac3-3967-5f92-bf6e-ae6524a36328" :step/math-academy-id 44870 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125626}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125629 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124648 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124211 :question/id #uuid "94c54743-ba74-51c5-9c59-777125bb76f6" :question/math-academy-id "e-12465"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "233eab4c-ab17-53a8-ae03-f6f28ac78922" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048555} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048786} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048883}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124820} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124823} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284417} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284420} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284421} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284422} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186367874} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186401419} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186434751} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186434752} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186434753} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186434760}] :knowledge-point/title "Classifying Local Extrema Using Eigenvalues"} :step/id #uuid "ab662d13-5448-5cfa-8e5b-f6bf4d245b75" :step/math-academy-id 44871 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125487}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125630 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125874 :tutorial/content "Recall the following definitions for single-variable functions:\n\n- A function $f(x)$ over the domain $D \\subseteq \\mathbb{R}$ has a global minimum point at $x=a$ if $f(x) \\geq f(a)$ for all $x \\in D.$\n\n- A function $f(x)$ over the domain $D \\subseteq \\mathbb{R}$ has a global maximum point at $x=a$ if $f(x) \\leq f(a)$ for all $x \\in D.$\n\nOn the function's graph, the global minimum and maximum are the lowest and highest points, respectively.\n\n![](images/63/633526565293f480a6a5fc45b87a70d38e946ae0f72a925f0074776e201a43a4.png)\n\nWe can generalize this concept to multivariable functions $f:\\mathbb R^n\\to\\mathbb R.$\n\n- A multivariable function $f(\\mathbf{x})$ with domain $D \\subseteq \\mathbb{R}^n$ has a **global minimum point** at $\\mathbf x = \\mathbf{a} \\in D$ if $f(\\mathbf{x}) \\geq f(\\mathbf{a})$ for all points $\\mathbf{x} \\in D.$ The value $f(\\mathbf{a})$ is the **global minimum** of the function, the smallest value the function attains on its domain.\n\n- A multivariable function $f(\\mathbf{x})$ with domain $D \\subseteq \\mathbb{R}^n$ has a **global maximum point** at $\\mathbf x = \\mathbf{a} \\in D$ if $f(\\mathbf{x}) \\leq f(\\mathbf{a})$ for all points $\\mathbf{x} \\in D.$ The value $f(\\mathbf{a})$ is the **global maximum** of the function, the largest value the function attains on its domain.\n\nIn particular, for a function $f : \\mathbb{R}^2 \\to \\mathbb{R},$ the global minimum and maximum are the lowest and highest points on the graph $z=f(x,y),$ respectively.\n\n![](images/de/de4cfc96fdce210430291999e5997f4a0c39ed1fbe63c3848b61508ca3f04114.png)\n\nFor the function shown above:\n\n- The minimum value reached by the function is $f(x,y) = -2,$ so the global minimum is $-2,$ The function reaches this value at $(x,y) = (1,-1).$ Therefore the global minimum point is $(1,-1,-2).$\n\n- Similarly, the maximum value reached by the function is $f(x,y) = 2,$ so the global maximum is $2.$ The function reaches this value at $(x,y) = (1,1).$ Therefore the global maximum point is $(1,1,2).$" :tutorial/id #uuid "c6c71de7-b70c-5b7f-a566-143fd897ff59" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7753 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "d367e806-9c4c-5d98-bc43-97c56882eee8" :step/math-academy-id 44876 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125625}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125645 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125885 :tutorial/content "To summarize, the point $\\mathbf{a} \\in \\mathbb{R}^n$ is a stationary point of the function $f : \\mathbb{R}^n \\to \\mathbb{R}$ if $\\boldsymbol{f}'(\\mathbf{a}) = \\mathbf 0^T,$ and we can classify $\\mathbf{a}$ as follows:\n\n- if $\\boldsymbol{f}''(\\mathbf{a})$ is positive definite, then $\\mathbf{a}$ is a local minimum\n\n- if $\\boldsymbol{f}''(\\mathbf{a})$ is negative definite, then $\\mathbf{a}$ is a local maximum\n\n- if $\\boldsymbol{f}''(\\mathbf{a})$ is neither positive-definite nor negative-definite, then $\\mathbf{a}$ is a saddle point" :tutorial/id #uuid "ab23ca4b-1bff-5c96-a4d2-62b72b8adff2" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7852 :tutorial/title "A Summary of Key Results"} :step/id #uuid "a55eef78-6bcb-5da4-be6c-3164a273066c" :step/math-academy-id 45322 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125629}}] :activity/title "Global vs. Local Extrema and Critical Points of Multivariable Functions" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186124736}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046660} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186445202} :activity/id #uuid "632f0c8c-4357-5396-aad3-4401d3471d8c" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049676 :topic/id #uuid "0a177390-6525-5d0c-8721-f7f75db39408" :topic/math-academy-id 1945} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186445199 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186367874 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "99f22169-b8a2-5fb3-aca1-3e5599415ad8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "local maximum" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367876} {:answer/id #uuid "abbaa7f2-5259-5b5a-bebd-07f255b181e9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "inconclusive" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367877} {:answer/id #uuid "f674340e-9bc4-5748-9997-f0404db95ca2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "not a critical point" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367878} {:answer/id #uuid "383240c1-34bf-5387-adaf-b9355e4cf27e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "local minimum" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367879} {:answer/id #uuid "0e483432-9f14-537e-b0dd-61a175cc1645" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "saddle point" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367880}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186367876} :answer-field/id #uuid "be669623-0526-55a8-9e58-06217378cfec" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367875}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "1f0502ee-0a83-5915-919b-cb8a0333bfec" :question/math-academy-id "q-163775" :question/problem "The quadratic Taylor approximation of the function $f(x)$ about $x=a\\in {R}^{2}$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)≈1+\\frac{1}{2}(x-a)^{T}[\\begin{aligned}-6 & 3 \\\\ 3 & -6\\end{aligned}](x-a).\n$$\n\nGiven that the eigenvalues of ${f}^{″}(a)$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=-3$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-9,$ what type of point is $a?$" :question/worked-solution "The point $a\\in {R}^{n}$ is a critical point of the function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R$ if ${f}^{′}(a)={0}^{T}.$ In such cases, we can classify $a$ as follows:\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is positive definite, then $a$ is a local minimum\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is negative definite, then $a$ is a local maximum\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is neither positive-definite nor negative-definite, then $a$ is a saddle point\n\nThe quadratic Taylor expansion of a function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R$ about $x=a\\in {R}^{n}$ given by\n\n$$\nf(x)≈f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)+\\frac{1}{2}(x-a)^{T}{f}^{″}(a)(x-a).\n$$\n\nSince the quadratic Taylor expansion of $f$ has no $(x-a)$ term, we can conclude that ${f}^{′}(a)={0}^{T}.$ Hence, $a$ is a critical point of $f.$\n\nFinally, notice that both the eigenvalues of ${f}^{″}(a)$ are negative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }_{1} & =-3<0 \\\\ {\\lambda }_{2} & =-9<0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the matrix is negative definite. Therefore, $a$ is a local maximum of $f.$"} :step/id #uuid "40399da2-21fd-5f08-bac2-f30a1d0cf8b3" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186445203}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186445200 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186445198 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "118339a7-669b-5081-8175-449ccce667f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "The function does not have local maxima" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445192} {:answer/id #uuid "80185868-d8d6-5a9a-9b8a-83d741afd936" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$B$ and $C$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445193} {:answer/id #uuid "b251a48a-e4be-5932-a217-f573cfba9f2d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$C$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445194} {:answer/id #uuid "b9b3e9e9-2073-5961-8289-000100206e82" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$B$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445195} {:answer/id #uuid "e2b33820-f35d-5434-abf3-958f348e4695" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$A$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445196}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186445196} :answer-field/id #uuid "261ceadd-9e63-5d86-9762-c5e81f0679da" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445197}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "da2e1b4b-fd99-52e9-91fa-fb0d43968219" :question/math-academy-id "q-164157" :question/problem "![](images/19/19f4f2d616fa648fcd4663be22b32ab18662fffa8e31f9bd44f0dd3b6da99208.png)\n\nThe graph of the function $z=f(x,y)$ is shown above. Which of the points shown are local maximum points?" :question/worked-solution "A function $f(x,y)$ has a local maximum point at $(a,b)$ if $f(x,y)\\le f(a,b)$ for all points $(x,y)$ in a neighborhood of $(a,b).$ \n\nIn other words, we can take a neighborhood of $(x,y)$-values such that the point $(x,y,f(x,y))$ is the highest point on the graph in the neighborhood.\n\nSuch region exists for the point $A.$ Hence, $A$ is a local maximum point.\n\n**Note**: For the point $A({x}_{A},{y}_{A},{z}_{A}),$ there are points $(x,y)$ in the surrounding regions such that $f({x}_{A},{y}_{A})=f(x,y).$ However, since the definition of local extrema is non-strict, $f(x,y)\\le f(a,b)$, the point $A$ does satisfy the conditions for a local maximum.\n\n![](images/f0/f0b16c2cb372280ec39915b0e8d4e396e68baec9a8e20edc5389ced079648836.png)"} :step/id #uuid "781a150f-a171-5196-8aca-b895a99d8faa" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186445201}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186445201 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284450 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "321b323f-56e0-55fb-b404-b19f30c4f96d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$(0,-2,-1.2)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282139} {:answer/id #uuid "cfae193b-da7f-5e1b-bf9e-4ef2f1177187" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$(2,3,0)$ and $(0,-2,-1.2)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283501} {:answer/id #uuid "d0e29d66-dcd3-589d-8fbe-6a8511fdfa3d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$(1,1,-2)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283513} {:answer/id #uuid "f4af8296-0c7f-540f-a7a3-bb0a43f520a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$(1,1,-2)$ and $(-2,1,3)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283827} {:answer/id #uuid "fadd78d8-5eef-5833-8275-ca89bd91ab2b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$(1,1,-2)$ and $(0,-2,-1.2)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283883}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186283513} :answer-field/id #uuid "b8ed0cd9-02bd-5986-85dc-65dabf690efc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284263}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "25f6d53a-4346-557b-a7bc-14685aa9ba47" :question/math-academy-id "q-164169" :question/problem "![](images/86/867fa59781c1732536e984398478996150c3d6b715c9d216bb82a35e0caf27e0.png)\n\nThe graph $z=f(x,y)$ is shown above. What are the global minimum points of the function?" :question/worked-solution "First, we recall the following definitions:\n\n- The global minimum of a function is the smallest value it attains on its domain. There can only be (at most) one global minimum.\n\n- A global minimum point is a point $(x,y,z)$ where a function reaches a global minimum. There can be zero, one, or many global minimum points.\n\nFor our function, the minimum value reached by the function is $f(x,y)=-2.$ Therefore, the global minimum is $-2.$ The function reaches this value when $(x,y)=(1,1),$ so the global minimum point is $(1,1,-2).$\n\n![](images/9e/9e2c084b2697ada9a957eec3629ac71aea150563a3af37b4f5d0b25e1921559a.png)"} :step/id #uuid "7aa4c459-af9e-558f-a889-0dc28bf20ece"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186445202 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124813 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "488efa2b-10b1-5ebf-8034-0b0da5c882dc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$A$ and $C$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367744} {:answer/id #uuid "98cb67f6-d724-5948-9425-0c038ad2edeb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$D$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367745} {:answer/id #uuid "15886344-1487-5168-91d8-69b1e7041eba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$B$ and $D$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367746} {:answer/id #uuid "eebf7d16-f087-53f4-ab00-09d21e4e2973" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "The function does not have local maximum" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367747} {:answer/id #uuid "048f3316-57b5-5780-82e6-d17e9b479998" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$B$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367748}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186367746} :answer-field/id #uuid "740b6c0e-c3bd-5817-9e86-f793c37cace9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367743}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "22b25e93-5d90-5fd1-8087-a5332a99d86f" :question/math-academy-id "q-162743" :question/problem "![](images/67/6715fe4c0f0b69044ee93da6d138a8d79cc9b509e7c912f79467d9f0b8e6d6be.png)\n\nThe graph of the function $z=f(x,y)$ is shown above. Which of the points shown are local maximum points?" :question/worked-solution "A function $f(x,y)$ has a local maximum point at $(a,b)$ if $f(x,y)\\le f(a,b)$ for all points $(x,y)$ in a neighborhood of $(a,b).$ \n\nIn other words, we can take a neighborhood of $(x,y)$-values such that the point $(x,y,f(x,y))$ is the highest point on the graph in the neighborhood.\n\nSuch neighborhoods exist for the points $B$ and $D.$ Hence $B$ and $D$ are local maxima.\n\n![](images/44/4464c66c00bc020668bef5e346caee352228403068be1334fc4997439140ae7f.png)"} :step/id #uuid "f285dabd-6305-5f61-b401-ef0b24f27f1a" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186445199}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186445203 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186401439 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "475ce117-63c5-5ee4-b359-fc20c9c51651" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "The function does not have local maxima" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401441} {:answer/id #uuid "cc880a4e-5176-5965-abb2-4d59cb691ec9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$D$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401442} {:answer/id #uuid "e2bd567a-a4c4-543c-81f0-2381cab6d5e8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$A$ and $B$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401443} {:answer/id #uuid "56368805-72a8-566e-a53d-bb48170c39e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$C$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401444} {:answer/id #uuid "41a0973a-78e5-5b21-83f7-bcf5f3ddb6d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$C$ and $D$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401445}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186401445} :answer-field/id #uuid "b8d87059-ea13-55bb-b541-23109862b322" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401440}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "2740d2d6-bc06-5779-9308-da4a9d560214" :question/math-academy-id "q-164152" :question/problem "![](images/c4/c460920f6b2516ac8603852e12d15840cca051736555ea43cf15a278ef9e0155.png)\n\nThe graph of the function $z=f(x,y)$ is shown above. Which of the points shown are local maximum points?" :question/worked-solution "A function $f(x,y)$ has a local maximum point at $(a,b)$ if $f(x,y)\\le f(a,b)$ for all points $(x,y)$ in a neighborhood of $(a,b).$ \n\nIn other words, we can take a neighborhood of $(x,y)$-values such that the point $(x,y,f(x,y))$ is the highest point on the graph in the neighborhood.\n\nSuch neighborhoods exist for the points $C$ and $D.$ Hence $C$ and $D$ are local maxima.\n\n![](images/cc/cc526bb1cf73fb5b5579966e91077ccd096aea475a4cf13196166bd65ac88d7b.png)"} :step/id #uuid "f301e8ab-1294-53d5-b248-897ded01ea54" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186445200}}] :activity/title "Global vs. Local Extrema and Critical Points of Multivariable Functions" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445191}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046644} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186506627} :activity/id #uuid "18d3d63c-6302-5918-86bd-56e3b125a276" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049676 :topic/id #uuid "0a177390-6525-5d0c-8721-f7f75db39408" :topic/math-academy-id 1945} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186506607 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186401426 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d12bfd22-9e9d-5d37-bbd0-35e563e5e30a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$(1,0,-2)$ and $(2,3,0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401428} {:answer/id #uuid "99f7cc57-ad3a-5493-ba20-b02d01c3c072" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$(0,-2,1.2)$  only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401429} {:answer/id #uuid "7d4d1de4-cb9d-5b3a-b919-1ee28c961db6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$(2,3,0)$  only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401430} {:answer/id #uuid "27f2d2ad-7cab-5c68-bfaf-a31ab980901d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$(-2,1,3)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401431} {:answer/id #uuid "d947cbda-24ab-58ef-b437-c399ba283d13" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$(0,-2,1.2)$ and $(-2,1,3)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401432}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186401431} :answer-field/id #uuid "32d7053b-a2c6-58ee-8efc-3a03178f6bd3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401427}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "8c163c8c-12fc-59a2-9e1d-9f264f8ad03a" :question/math-academy-id "q-163834" :question/problem "![](images/21/215b322e06280dd01e5858df9a17cd56673b5cdec69521decca205e03771ac21.png)\n\nThe graph $z=f(x,y)$ is shown above. What are the global maximum points of the function?" :question/worked-solution "First, we recall the following definitions:\n\n- The global maximum of a function is the largest value it attains on its domain. There can only be (at most) one global maximum.\n\n- A global maximum point is a point $(x,y,z)$ where a function reaches a global maximum. There can be zero, one, or many global maximum points.\n\nFor our function, the maximum value reached by the function is $f(x,y)=3.$ Therefore, the global maximum is $3.$ The function reaches this value when $(x,y)=(-2,1),$ so the global maximum point is $(-2,1,3).$\n\n![](images/d5/d5f948483a80f37257b285d48cd1e6bbf1d8a6ba9b66d1fea5edd865c787711a.png)"} :step/id #uuid "1354966a-bc66-58aa-a442-7d67b1cd669b" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186506632}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186506618 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186434756 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5d6a8563-51e7-5f75-b1f9-5030c16e40a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$A$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434680} {:answer/id #uuid "671887d0-2723-5a15-b0f2-b1adf3728767" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$A$ and $D$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434684} {:answer/id #uuid "7eb013d8-4bec-59b6-be78-16c6b4278be3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "The function does not have local minima" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434688} {:answer/id #uuid "7ec46caa-3ab4-5a82-8cef-51349b889f2b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$B$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434689} {:answer/id #uuid "c579e011-3bca-5b23-92a7-a4c2a45142bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$C$ and $D$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434716}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186434684} :answer-field/id #uuid "caaf0180-b775-51a5-8357-5dbad212c145" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434747}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "73e92678-31f3-525b-9096-a06391739cab" :question/math-academy-id "q-164151" :question/problem "![](images/f7/f7429a2fc291fc9e14685208288f670cca341a260ac13b358c52d99c35e13b4f.png)\n\nThe graph of the function $z=f(x,y)$ is shown above. Which of the points shown are local minimum points?" :question/worked-solution "A function $f(x,y)$ has a local minimum point at $(a,b)$ if $f(x,y)\\ge f(a,b)$ for all points $(x,y)$ in a neighborhood of $(a,b).$ \n\nIn other words, we can take a neighborhood of $(x,y)$-values such that the point $(x,y,f(x,y))$ is the lowest point on the graph in the neighborhood.\n\nSuch neighborhoods exist for the points $A$ and $D.$ Hence, $A$ and $D$ are local minima.\n\n![](images/b8/b8952aa59c9351c9723cd62f35073d46d7ddfd46afa9a9b3ddae7ce149065c59.png)"} :step/id #uuid "3895ce48-ecf8-5610-add7-df9917d32e51" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186506626}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186506626 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186434761 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2683d2d1-d298-565b-a7c7-9a4e0ec1b79e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$A$ and $B$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434665} {:answer/id #uuid "3674a427-b1f6-5423-9cad-a53248cc1526" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$B$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434668} {:answer/id #uuid "3af27a0e-03ce-5737-81df-4b4e8b4ce792" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$C$ and $D$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434669} {:answer/id #uuid "867414de-477d-5e96-ac86-a8231e8e612f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$A$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434691} {:answer/id #uuid "ecbd6041-7dad-5acb-839a-6742c3614749" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "The function does not have local maxima" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434727}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186434668} :answer-field/id #uuid "2bbf6504-629f-56a6-8d02-ea8b2cdc4409" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434737}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "c719ba47-2cb2-55e7-85ae-fa0a3ade984e" :question/math-academy-id "q-164153" :question/problem "![](images/79/79971602dd6834147c3db9361c94853a5a66c8e899978df3629d2841326fe62c.png)\n\nThe graph of the function $z=f(x,y)$ is shown above. Which of the points shown are local maximum points?" :question/worked-solution "A function $f(x,y)$ has a local maximum point at $(a,b)$ if $f(x,y)\\le f(a,b)$ for all points $(x,y)$ in a neighborhood of $(a,b).$ \n\nIn other words, we can take a neighborhood of $(x,y)$-values such that the point $(x,y,f(x,y))$ is the highest point on the graph in the neighborhood.\n\nSuch neighborhood exists for the point $B.$ Hence, $B$ is a local maximum.\n\n![](images/68/685c03b86db93032c8a79334c5fa46c561dd263a9aa4f52b9d7e6f41c3bb827c.png)"} :step/id #uuid "5c591abe-52fa-53e7-961d-0fc811191f5e"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186506627 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124811 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "40d51ffd-3c87-5270-834d-5cb8714970fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$(1,2,2)$ and $(-1,-2,1)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434670} {:answer/id #uuid "87e76412-257f-5583-a7e9-47e4ed2af022" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$(1,2,2)$ and $(0,-1,0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434692} {:answer/id #uuid "acec2003-faf8-5414-9547-98be2232672